// The revl stdlib VERSION STAMP (roadmap item 389).
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------- why
// A consumer that VENDORS the stdlib holds byte-COPIES of `stdlib/*.rvl` in its
// own tree (the revl-harness copies `stdlib/{json,value,str}.rvl`). Those copies
// go stale silently: when item 104 added `value_is_object` to `stdlib/value.rvl`
// upstream, the compiler began recommending `value_is_object` as a fix — but the
// harness's byte-copy predated that symbol, so the recommended API did not exist
// in the consumer's tree, and nothing noticed. There was no way for the consumer
// to ask "is my copy of the stdlib the one this compiler expects?".
//
// This module IS that marker. It is a single, pure-revl, zero-argument fn that
// returns the stdlib's version as a `Str`. Because it is pure revl (no `@py`
// bodies) it lowers on every tier, so a consumer can call it on any backend and
// print/compare the result. The version travels WITH the file: a vendored copy
// carries the version it was copied AT, and the compiler ships (and knows) the
// version it EXPECTS — the two disagree exactly when the copy has drifted.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------- how to read it
// A consumer holding a vendored copy compares two numbers:
//   * what my copy IS      — call `stdlib_version()` from the vendored copy;
//   * what the compiler WANTS — the compiler's `EXPECTED_STDLIB_VERSION`
//     (src/revl/stdlib_version.py), surfaced by `revl doctor`, which warns when
//     the resolved stdlib's stamp differs from the version the compiler expects.
// See docs/stdlib-version.md for the consumer-side recipe and the drift check.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------ BUMP DISCIPLINE
// Bump the integer below by one whenever a PUBLIC stdlib symbol is ADDED,
// REMOVED, or CHANGED in signature/behaviour — i.e. any change a consumer's
// vendored copy could fall behind. Item 104's `value_is_object` is the canonical
// example: adding it is exactly the kind of change that must bump this stamp, so
// a downstream copy predating it reads back an older number and is flagged as
// drifted. A change that touches only comments or a private (non-`pub`) helper
// need not bump. The stamp is a monotonic counter, NOT semver: it only has to
// increase, so "newer than expected" and "older than expected" are both
// distinguishable from "in sync".
pub fn stdlib_version() -> Str {
  return "1"
}
